(/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/27b99f04c6f0f2b24f2d107248a2315f6b2d2574f849bf47379d8f2fdd27d05e.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/b758dc5e36b9ce2daba82e94d58b5232cecff327cf85f3a2571675c5845ba90d.png) and divide by the total number of marbles in the mixture ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/75c92641ef9ece42d3dab493a8e8fd78d276cd4f40e423f2b442d23464fb47c7.png) So,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/6ddd96143aa9754eb6b266d82b854a41fcd3e17d9d9c5aeec7d44a669e1994b3.png)\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marble is green is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/a1fb9fe4e3ce980bd3385ece84df7522afee0f98b5e57cf82e8c7e0a19a4119c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083033 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "f13f7b98-a9fc-5b9f-ad44-59d5ead049a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7865" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components, where $70\\%$ of the components are produced by machine $A,$ and the remaining $30\\%$ are produced by machine $B.$ Of the components produced by machine $A,$ $8\\%$ are defective, and of the components produced by both machines, $11\\%$ are defective. If a component produced by machine $B$ is randomly selected, what is the probability of being defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A.$ Then $A'$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D) = P(D|A)P(A) + P(D|A')P(A')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $70\\%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $30\\%$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A) = 0.70, \\quad P(A') = 0.30.\n$$\n\n- Since $8\\%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A) = 0.08.\n$$\n\n- Since $11\\%$ of the components produced by both machines are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D) = 0.11.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|A'),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.11 & =(0.08)(0.70)+P(D|{A}^{′})(0.30) \\\\ 0.110 & =0.056+0.3P(D|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.3P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.054 \\\\ P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $B$ is defective is $0.18.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07eb336-1ac8-5633-abaf-fcfca4c12bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Intersections"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a670870-2343-5277-b441-bde35d4095a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383769} {:answer/id #uuid "0feba903-8267-54b7-9df4-15dbc5a8e353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383770} {:answer/id #uuid "0802490b-c4c2-55b2-ab92-28c76b11d624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383771} {:answer/id #uuid "e918507c-cf03-5757-82fb-222ad862d1b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383772} {:answer/id #uuid "fc461954-364c-5ac9-9512-f69d1908ffa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc461954-364c-5ac9-9512-f69d1908ffa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383773} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f79430e-9462-5ae8-9b51-a3eb667f495c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6943a89b-51ba-5049-a4c3-036341b2c35b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122306" :question/problem "In a class, $20$ students are boys, and $24$ are girls. $5%$ of the boys are engaged in a sports section, and $25%$ of the girls are engaged in a sports section. What is the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section?" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the selected student is engaged in a sports section, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap {B}^{′})\n$$\n\nThere are $20$ boys in the class, and $5%$ of them are engaged in a sports section. So, the number of boys engaged in a sports section is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{100}\\cdot 20=1.\n$$\n\nThere are $24$  girls in the class, and $25%$ of them are engaged in a sports section. So, the number of girls  engaged in a sports section is\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{100}\\cdot 24=6.\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(S\\cap B),$ we take the number of boys engaged in a sports section $(1)$ and divide it by the total number of students in the class $(20+24=44).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap B)=\\frac{1}{44}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(S\\cap {B}^{′}),$ we take the number of girls engaged in a sports section $(6)$ and divide it by the total number of students in the class $(44).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}^{′})=\\frac{6}{44}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap {B}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{44}+\\frac{6}{44} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{44}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07eb336-1ac8-5633-abaf-fcfca4c12bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Intersections"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cb3b473-683e-52a1-aa46-beb6d66c6746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383776} {:answer/id #uuid "ecedb263-cddb-5a5f-b6b4-09b85c85846f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383777} {:answer/id #uuid "9c481742-707b-5237-884e-167338ca5202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383778} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9151d2-dbfd-506f-8060-8af36f5c0f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383779} {:answer/id #uuid "ab597d41-4a4e-56cf-ab99-5bf49aec01bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c481742-707b-5237-884e-167338ca5202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383778} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a7bf08-7f81-528b-a7c5-d4b0e9790349" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76e576c7-40ca-500f-b543-78612835b8f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-122397" :question/problem "Among the people arrested in a certain city in the mid-1990s, $18%$ were males under $18$ years old and $54%$ were males of $18$ years old and over. What is the probability that a person selected at random from all those arrested is a male?" :question/worked-solution "Let $M$ be the event that the chosen person is a male, and let $U$ be the event that the chosen person is under $18.$ Then, ${U}^{′}$ is the event that the person is $18$ or over.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person is a male, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(M)=P(M\\cap U)+P(M\\cap {U}^{′})\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nP(M\\cap U)=0.18,\\,P(M\\cap {U}^{′})=0.54.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person is a male is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M) & =P(M\\cap U)+P(M\\cap {U}^{′}) \\\\  & =0.18+0.54 \\\\  & =0.72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "378b1b33-8e5c-5342-85bc-76e07be00442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383783} {:answer/id #uuid "96902438-963c-5218-adf7-cd6a7a46e7f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383784} {:answer/id #uuid "6906ee86-bfa3-5739-9c54-667b86c46fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383785} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d45c5a-ae2b-50e8-906d-666f6b1282f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383786} {:answer/id #uuid "6536a8d6-bc51-54f9-a0d4-baadba843bcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6906ee86-bfa3-5739-9c54-667b86c46fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383785} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b9ef9dc-7a01-5439-9c40-638f91b5375c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa6ce446-8372-563e-b2d4-b6a175008dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122403" :question/problem "Two machines ${M}_{1}$ and ${M}_{2}$ in a particular factory produce $75%$ and $25%,$ respectively, of the total daily output of electronic tubes. It is known that $4%$ of the tubes produced by ${M}_{1}$ are defective and that the probability that a randomly selected tube from a day's production is defective is $0.0375.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected tube produced by ${M}_{2}$ is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected tube is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the tube is produced by machine ${M}_{1}.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the tube is produced by machine ${M}_{2}.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected tube from a day's production is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine ${M}_{1}$ produces $75%$ of the total daily output of electronic tubes and ${M}_{2}$ the remaining $25%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.75,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $4%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{1}$ are defective,  we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected tube from a day's production is defective is $0.0375,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.0375.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|{A}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.0375 & =(0.04)(0.75)+P(D|{A}^{′})(0.25) \\\\ 0.0375 & =0.03+0.25P(D|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.0075 & =0.25P(D|{A}^{′}) \\\\ P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected tube produced by ${M}_{2}$ is defective is $0.03.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70dd2be3-17ea-5aa0-998e-febe1f3b4c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383790} {:answer/id #uuid "4631437a-9b6b-5e0d-9d15-50b03bcfd0d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383791} {:answer/id #uuid "94590f98-8712-5a8e-a21d-76ed66d3694c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383792} {:answer/id #uuid "02e76174-bd01-533b-b73f-5c9e5c5849f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383793} {:answer/id #uuid "a41b2f5c-cbff-5d4d-bd01-09c4135a062f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02e76174-bd01-533b-b73f-5c9e5c5849f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383793} :answer-field/id #uuid "321bd09c-e3d3-5497-9946-e7eda90a95f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d44f26f0-f74d-5614-a0df-014aa1f39a6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122412" :question/problem "Company $A$ supplies $80%$ of widgets for a car shop and only $1%$ of their widgets turn out to be defective. Company $B$ supplies the remaining $20%$ of widgets. The probability that a widget from the car shop will be defective is $0.014.$ If a customer randomly purchases a widget supplied by company $B,$ what is the probability that it will be defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a widget will be defective, and let $A$ be the event that the widget is supplied by company $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the widget is supplied by company $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly purchased widget will be defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since company $A$ supplies $80%$ of widgets for a car shop and company $B$ the remaining $20%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.80,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.20.\n$$\n\n- Since $1%$ of the widget supplied by company $A$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.01.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a widget from the car shop is defective will be $0.014,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.014.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|{A}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.014 & =(0.01)(0.80)+P(D|{A}^{′})(0.20) \\\\ 0.014 & =0.008+0.20P(D|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.20P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.006 \\\\ P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected widget supplied by company $B$ will be defective is $0.03.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ce8d5b6-8767-5a9e-942b-c2f29d766905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082563} {:answer/id #uuid "2b721a0f-fc76-5606-a2d2-0af29b118408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082564} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca486b5-ec22-5985-a1dc-2c118dfedec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082565} {:answer/id #uuid "4df1eb91-c8a1-5cd8-bf6a-612ce3352e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082566} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2b8630-7a2f-58a8-a8fc-b98cb4320e08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4df1eb91-c8a1-5cd8-bf6a-612ce3352e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082566} :answer-field/id #uuid "d81d1bde-9fd7-5105-9f29-22ffdfe3e30a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ce848d4-59c3-57f3-83e1-aacd036756e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-88578" :question/problem "In a class, $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the students are boys, and $\\frac{2}{3}$ are girls. Of the boys, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of them play soccer, and of the girls, $\\frac{1}{4}$ of them play soccer. What is the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen student plays soccer, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|B)P(B)+P(S|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the students are boys and $\\frac{2}{3}$ are girls, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P({B}^{′})=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the boys play soccer and $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the girls play soccer, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|B)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,P(S|{B}^{′})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|B)P(B)+P(S|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07eb336-1ac8-5633-abaf-fcfca4c12bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Intersections"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e16ff51-48c5-57e3-89a6-9d9d895369b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383797} {:answer/id #uuid "0b50dd2e-223c-5bee-a71e-8e2f3abad80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383798} {:answer/id #uuid "3023f36c-eb25-51ae-a411-c717ad0db959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383799} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb5b3a4-a852-5123-91fd-6df2bf43bc16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383800} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdb937b-4541-5ad6-8a20-01a03b4b1c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b50dd2e-223c-5bee-a71e-8e2f3abad80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383798} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2c9694e-34c6-5f3f-8b2d-d21304c9cc80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95918580-9eee-5598-9f0d-4111cb92fbec" :question/math-academy-id "q-88830" :question/problem "In a class, $17$ students are boys, and $23$ are girls. Of the boys, $2$ belong to the school chess club. Of the girls, $3$ are in the chess club. What is the probability that a randomly selected student in the class is in the chess club?" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen student is on the chess club, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student is on the chess club, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C\\cap B)+P(C\\cap {B}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(C\\cap B),$ we take the number of students in the class who are on the chess club and are boys $(2)$ and divide by the total number of students in the class $(17+23=40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap B)=\\frac{2}{40}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(C\\cap {B}^{′}),$ we take the number of students in the class who are on the chess club and are girls $(3)$ and divide by the total number of students in the class $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap {B}^{′})=\\frac{3}{40}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student is on the chess club is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap B)+P(C\\cap {B}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{40}+\\frac{3}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07eb336-1ac8-5633-abaf-fcfca4c12bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Intersections"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6e5f31-2ef6-5826-b822-30771464fb4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383804} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c84331-3f1e-5da2-8c93-026e1f2c4a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383805} {:answer/id #uuid "bb10ad24-7a0b-5f05-99d2-925d73c1553b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383806} {:answer/id #uuid "b265767a-d892-530b-a675-91707afd969b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383807} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c48099-365c-527a-a489-6b77feda06e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b265767a-d892-530b-a675-91707afd969b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383807} :answer-field/id #uuid "4882a8ae-982e-563f-9966-d6be916e01ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f027c79-6eb0-5320-9fc8-81849b6c264f" :question/math-academy-id "q-88892" :question/problem "Alice has two email accounts, $A$ and $B.$ In account $A,$ she received $24$ emails of which $5$ are spam. In the account $B,$ she received $18$ emails of which $2$ are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email in either account is spam?" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen email is spam, and let $A$ be the event that the email was received in account $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the email was received in account $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap A)+P(S\\cap {A}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(S\\cap A),$ we take the number of emails in account A that are spam $(5)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(24+18=42).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap A)=\\frac{5}{42}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(S\\cap {A}^{′}),$ we take the number of emails in account $B$ that are spam $(2)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(24+18=42).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {A}^{′})=\\frac{2}{42}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email is spam is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap A)+P(S\\cap {A}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{42}+\\frac{2}{42} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{42} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07eb336-1ac8-5633-abaf-fcfca4c12bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Intersections"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "824b12f8-20c6-5453-a979-a729eaaee723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383811} {:answer/id #uuid "2f577ca1-c818-5e5e-a3e2-271bbaa936d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383812} {:answer/id #uuid "a090565b-1a19-5a44-989c-0755c93fd0ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383813} {:answer/id #uuid "66ac889a-eb70-5b87-9794-de4263c4241f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383814} {:answer/id #uuid "64c11af1-38ec-5056-85d3-f7f60287e49f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "824b12f8-20c6-5453-a979-a729eaaee723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383811} :answer-field/id #uuid "e326adc8-e341-55a0-b4b0-956bab5b1d3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0ecdd68-13a0-5025-b2d2-94155866cbce" :question/math-academy-id "q-88922" :question/problem "A gas station offers two types of gasoline, regular and premium. On a certain day, of the $43$ customers who bought regular gas, $23$ paid with a credit card. On the same day, of the $38$ customers that bought premium gas, $31$ of them paid with a credit card. What is the probability of a randomly selected customer paying with a credit card?" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person paid with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person bought regular gas. Then ${R}^{′}$ is the event that the person bought premium gas.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap {R}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(C\\cap R),$ we take the number of people who bought regular gas and paid with a credit card $(23)$ and divide by the total number of customers on that day $(43+38=81).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap R)=\\frac{23}{81}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(C\\cap {R}^{′}),$ we take the number of people who bought premium gas and paid with a credit card $(31)$ and divide by the total number of customers on that day $(81).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap {R}^{′})=\\frac{31}{81}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap {R}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{81}+\\frac{31}{81} \\\\  & =\\frac{54}{81} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a29caa-0474-5e8d-a7cf-07a5a46279dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383818} {:answer/id #uuid "a5eecca0-4a81-5159-9815-0a0e4c3552cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383819} {:answer/id #uuid "03d3e4c7-7e88-5d61-91aa-54699b28a5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383820} {:answer/id #uuid "a2556848-ff90-5916-b237-9f6036542386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383821} {:answer/id #uuid "5bff7b5d-fde0-5ef4-a262-9b2f058cc694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01a29caa-0474-5e8d-a7cf-07a5a46279dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383818} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d84c8f8-d6ad-56c7-881d-b4732341da43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e8c8220-3427-51b3-8588-a7be73c3dc45" :question/math-academy-id "q-88977" :question/problem "A student answers a multiple-choice test. Each question of the test has four possible answers, and only one choice is correct. The student knows the correct answer for $60%$ of the questions. If the student decides to select an answer at random for the questions that she does not know the answer to, what is the probability that a randomly selected question on the test is correct?" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that the selected question is correct, and let $K$ be the event that the student knew the answer. Then ${K}^{′}$ is the event that the student did not know the answer.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected question is correct, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{′})P({K}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the student knows the answer to $60%$ of the questions, we have\n\n$$\nP(K)=0.6,\\,P({K}^{′})=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since each question has four possible answers, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|K)=1,\\,P(C|{K}^{′})=\\frac{1}{4}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected question on the test is correct is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{′})P({K}^{′}) \\\\  & =(1)(0.6)+(0.25)(0.4) \\\\  & =0.6+0.1 \\\\  & =0.70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92062e57-72f5-52d5-9bc3-e5ea12f69340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082599} {:answer/id #uuid "c40b53ad-a9ff-5932-ae33-21f4d952af3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.715" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082600} {:answer/id #uuid "df27d7a0-b3e8-5022-a045-ea9bf3c99ccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082601} {:answer/id #uuid "23639faf-2978-5b08-9630-bbf3a4155aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.521" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082602} {:answer/id #uuid "6b562a27-1dff-5ebb-a9f5-709bdcef7884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.215" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b562a27-1dff-5ebb-a9f5-709bdcef7884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.215" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082603} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d50d42-425e-5aa0-b1ca-9dd8d319a7da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d44e7ca-63d4-5d40-926f-44ff465c510b" :question/math-academy-id "q-88980" :question/problem "Sophia has two email accounts, account $A$ and account $B.$ She receives $35%$ of her emails in account $A,$ and the remaining $65%$ go into account $B.$ Approximately $15%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam while $25%$ of the emails received in account $B$ are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email is spam?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen email is spam, and let $A$ be the event that the email selected is from account $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the email selected is from account $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of the emails are received in account $A$ and the remaining $65%$ in account $B$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $15%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam while $25%$ of the emails received in account $B$ are spam, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|A)=0.15,\\,P(S|{A}^{′})=0.25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email is spam is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.15)(0.35)+(0.25)(0.65) \\\\  & =0.215.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab3c0f67-fddf-55d9-bd45-dd88f4b249b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383825} {:answer/id #uuid "28f8e32b-32c6-585a-bd63-ddbc1bea88ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383826} {:answer/id #uuid "80e8e961-53e9-5144-b814-84be4d81580b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383827} {:answer/id #uuid "b15ea9f0-6569-556b-bff3-708a2d847938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383828} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c9143e-4f71-563d-b094-54ce9bb65b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b15ea9f0-6569-556b-bff3-708a2d847938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383828} :answer-field/id #uuid "74879bd6-5325-5128-a562-4a6802ea22ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "408466b2-d5d7-5a6f-92f2-2e2d6c4b81d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-89031" :question/problem "A certain disease is present in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of the population. A test to detect the disease gives a positive result in $\\frac{9}{10}$ of the people with the disease and in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of the people without the disease. What is the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive for the disease?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that the test is positive for the selected patient, and let $D$ be the event that the patient has the disease. Then, ${D}^{′}$ is the event that the patient does not have the disease.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected patient gives a positive result, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(A|D)P(D)+P(A|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the disease is present in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of the population, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=\\frac{1}{10},\\,P({D}^{′})=\\frac{9}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the test gives a positive result in $\\frac{9}{10}$ of the people with the disease, and in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of the people without the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D)=\\frac{9}{10},\\,P(A|{D}^{′})=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive for the disease is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A|D)P(D)+P(A|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{10})(\\frac{1}{10})+(\\frac{1}{10})(\\frac{9}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{100}+\\frac{9}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "961152f7-bee5-558f-bce0-6ef9f0c80ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383832} {:answer/id #uuid "be3c9adf-bc12-52f3-a569-7a970a8a76bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.197" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383833} {:answer/id #uuid "8642fbe2-99a1-5834-97ce-6dcc27e3977a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.227" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383834} {:answer/id #uuid "87ab7379-f694-5997-b5bb-f6d10fe3535e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383835} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4b2075-eb10-5e2e-a960-abbdce3f9921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.312" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "961152f7-bee5-558f-bce0-6ef9f0c80ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383832} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdf28614-efc7-52f0-bd6a-d1efea9c55ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "846490bd-6254-5a08-8e31-cc536ddf844b" :question/math-academy-id "q-89041" :question/problem "A math teacher split their calculus class into two groups, Group $A$ and Group $B.$ Group $A$ has $43%$ of the total number of students, while Group $B$ has $57%$ of the total number of students. If $23%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, and $19%$ of the students in Group $B$ obtained the top grade, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade?" :question/worked-solution "Let $G$ be the event that the chosen student obtained the top grade, and let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group $A.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the student is from Group $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $43%$ of their students are in Group $A$ and $57%$ are in Group $B$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.43,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.57.\n$$\n\n- Since $23%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade and $19%$ of the students in Group $B$ obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.23,\\,P(G|{A}^{′})=0.19.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.23)(0.43)+(0.19)(0.57) \\\\  & ≈0.207\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa45848-efd9-5d23-a679-2315501d86c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383839} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4e8b2e-23f0-51fa-8bbd-c91a4a75ba9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383840} {:answer/id #uuid "c26868d0-20af-533e-acc3-1b08c868e97f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383841} {:answer/id #uuid "cd38b16a-8193-5ee8-a86e-07cbc5bed587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383842} {:answer/id #uuid "990519d1-feee-5178-9796-c20ad96631c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c26868d0-20af-533e-acc3-1b08c868e97f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383841} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a91ee7-75c1-5c81-9b3e-691604156c90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b38f87cd-849d-519c-9592-01abf17dbe8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-89042" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the total components manufactured, $60%$ of them are produced by machine $A,$ and the remaining $40%$ are produced by machine $B.$ Of the components produced by machine $A,$ $10%$ are defective. In total, $12%$ of all components produced are defective. If a component produced by machine $B$ is selected at random, what is the probability of it being defective? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $60%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $40%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.60,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.40.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.10.\n$$\n\n- Since $12%$ of the components produced are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.12.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|{A}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.12 & =(0.10)(0.60)+P(D|{A}^{′})(0.40) \\\\ 0.12 & =0.06+0.40P(D|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.40P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.06 \\\\ P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $B$ is defective is $0.15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0436c47e-08d5-5b11-a423-5e72c7ed4d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383846} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9ac833-3359-5a89-8200-baf67ed28b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383847} {:answer/id #uuid "3c6ae10f-41b2-5de1-abe1-40985dad7c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383848} {:answer/id #uuid "156cfc01-9456-56a6-9143-c47d3248a744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383849} {:answer/id #uuid "12a2598b-b029-5a5c-a9dd-b8d78497567d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c6ae10f-41b2-5de1-abe1-40985dad7c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383848} :answer-field/id #uuid "f522b0f2-f7d7-56bf-aee8-db36961299fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9f71c3d4-298d-5849-a475-518d54df8b50" :question/math-academy-id "q-89053" :question/problem "In a gas station, $55%$ of the customers buy regular gasoline while $45%$ buy premium gasoline. If $60%$ of the customers that buy regular gasoline usually pay with a credit card and the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $0.645,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected customer who buys premium gasoline usually pays with a credit card? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person pays with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person buys regular gas. Then ${R}^{′}$ is the event that the person buys premium gas.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person pays with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $55%$ of the customers buy regular gasoline while $45%$ buy premium gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.55,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $60%$ of the customers that buy regular gasoline pay with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.60.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $0.645,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.645.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(C|{R}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′}) \\\\ 0.645 & =(0.60)(0.55)+P(C|{R}^{′})(0.45) \\\\ 0.645 & =0.33+0.45P(C|{R}^{′}) \\\\ 0.45P(C|{R}^{′}) & =0.315 \\\\ P(C|{R}^{′}) & =0.70.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected customer who buys premium gasoline usually pays with a credit card is $0.70.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9be8101-853c-5e90-bbf4-9dc1cc33fa29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082605} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4915a3-3555-5c10-9feb-6387352c3f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082606} {:answer/id #uuid "f717d1a1-6d07-5492-9bf5-c16f93fc198b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082607} {:answer/id #uuid "49a609c0-2540-5173-979a-90b54d9d39e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082608} {:answer/id #uuid "7f403e67-26d3-5981-abeb-ca9f111577e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f403e67-26d3-5981-abeb-ca9f111577e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082609} :answer-field/id #uuid "56eab7d9-7f5b-5bfe-b67b-db5ea19e8613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b31dbf65-1f9b-5a11-91ad-3e7a54aebef7" :question/math-academy-id "q-89054" :question/problem "A math teacher splits her calculus class into two groups, Group $A$ and Group $B.$ Of the total students, $35%$ of her students are in Group $A,$ and $65%$ are in Group $B.$ If $20%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, and the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade is $0.265,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected student from Group $B$ obtained the top grade? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $G$ be the event that the chosen student obtained the top grade, and let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the student is from Group $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of her students are in Group $A$ and $65%$ are in Group $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.20.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top  grade is $0.265,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=0.265.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G|{A}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.265 & =(0.20)(0.35)+P(G|{A}^{′})(0.65) \\\\ 0.265 & =0.07+0.65P(G|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.65P(G|{A}^{′}) & =0.195 \\\\ P(G|{A}^{′}) & =0.30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student from Group $B$ obtained the top grade is $0.30.$"}]}
